e at least two rectangles are required.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound (≥ 2) and the explicit construction (≤ 2) coincide, so the minimum number of tiles is exactly two.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of uncovered squares satisfying “exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column’’ determines a bijection  \n\n\\[\n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\qquad\n\\text{the uncovered square in row }i\\text{ is }(i,\\pi(i)).\n\\]\n\nThus the set of uncovered squares is the set  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA **tile** is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose vertices lie on grid points; it therefore corresponds to a pair of intervals  \n\n\\[\nR=[a,b]\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad \nC=[c,d]\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nand covers exactly the unit squares \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in R,\\;j\\in C\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (shape of a feasible rectangle)\n\nLet \\(R=[a,b]\\) be a non‑empty interval of rows.  \nA rectangle with row interval \\(R\\) can avoid all holes in \\(U\\) **iff** its column interval \\(C\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nC\\subseteq\\bigl[1,\\;\\min_{i\\in R}\\pi(i)-1\\bigr]\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\nC\\subseteq\\bigl[\\max_{i\\in R}\\pi(i)+1,\\;n\\bigr].\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a column \\(j\\) belongs to \\(C\\) and there exists \\(i\\in R\\) with \\(\\pi(i)=j\\), then the square \\((i,j)\\) would be both covered (by the rectangle) and uncovered (by definition of \\(U\\)), contradicting the rules. Hence \\(C\\) must avoid all values \\(\\pi(i)\\) with \\(i\\in R\\).  \nBecause \\(\\pi(i)\\) are integers, the set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\pi(i)\\mid i\\in R\\}\\) consists of two (possibly empty) intervals: everything left of the smallest \\(\\pi(i)\\) and everything right of the largest \\(\\pi(i)\\). Any interval \\(C\\) avoiding all \\(\\pi(i)\\) must be contained in one of those two intervals, which is precisely (2). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Consequence\n\nFor a fixed row interval \\(R\\) we never need more than **two** rectangles to cover all cells of those rows: one rectangle with column interval on the left side of (2) and, if the right side is non‑empty, a second rectangle on the right side.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Upper bound: construction with two tiles\n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nPick any integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) (e.g. \\(k=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)).  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_1&=[1,k], & C_1&=[k+1,n],\\\\[2pt]\nR_2&=[k+1,n], & C_2&=[1,k].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBoth pairs \\((R_1,C_1)\\) and \\((R_2,C_2)\\) satisfy Lemma 1 because for rows in \\(R_1\\) we have \\(\\pi(i)=i\\le k\\), so \\(\\max_{i\\in R_1}\\pi(i)=k\\) and \\(C_1\\) lies strictly to the right of this maximum; similarly for \\(R_2\\) the column interval \\(C_2\\) lies strictly to the left of the minimum \\(\\pi(i)=i\\ge k+1\\).\n\nHence the two rectangles\n\n\\[\nT_1=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in R_1,\\;j\\in C_1\\},\\qquad\nT_2=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in R_2,\\;j\\in C_2\\}\n\\]\n\nare admissible tiles.  \n\nEvery unit square not on the diagonal belongs either to the right of the diagonal (so it lies in \\(T_1\\)) or to the left of the diagonal (so it lies in \\(T_2\\)).  \nThe diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) are precisely the uncovered squares required by the problem.  \n\nThus **two** tiles suffice.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lower bound: at least two tiles are necessary\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a single tile \\(T\\) covers all covered squares.  \nLet its row interval be \\(R=[a,b]\\).  \nBecause each row contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(R\\) must be the whole set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); otherwise the rows outside 