ost \\(|\\{i\\mid u_j(i)=1\\}|+|\\{k\\mid v_j(k)=1\\}|\\) distinct pairs,\n   because a pair \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) can be covered either by the “row‑omitted’’ part\n   \\(\\{i\\mid u_j(i)=1\\}\\) or by the “column‑omitted’’ part \\(\\{k\\mid v_j(k)=1\\}\\), but never by both simultaneously (otherwise the biclique would contain the forbidden edge \\((i,\\pi(i))\\)).*\n\n   *Consequently the total number of pairs that can be covered by \\(m\\) bicliques is at most*\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{j=1}^m\\bigl(|\\overline R_j|+|\\overline C_j|\\bigr).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *Every row \\(i\\) must be omitted in at least one biclique (otherwise column \\(\\pi(i)\\) would be present in all bicliques and the edge \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) would be covered). Hence*\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{j=1}^m |\\overline R_j|\\ge n .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   *Similarly every column \\(\\pi(i)\\) must be omitted in at least one biclique, giving*\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{j=1}^m |\\overline C_j|\\ge n .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   *Adding (6) and (7) yields*\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{j=1}^m\\bigl(|\\overline R_j|+|\\overline C_j|\\bigr)\\ge 2n .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   *Because a single biclique can omit at most one row **and** one column simultaneously (otherwise it would contain a forbidden edge), we have*\n   \\[\n   |\\overline R_j|+|\\overline C_j|\\le 2\\qquad\\text{for every }j .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   *Combining (8) and (9) we obtain*\n   \\[\n   2m\\ge 2n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;m\\ge n .\n   \\]\n\n   *A sharper observation is that a biclique cannot omit **both** a row and a column that belong to the same matching edge, because then that edge would be absent from the union (2) and could not be covered at all. Therefore at most one of the two numbers \\(|\\overline R_j|\\) and \\(|\\overline C_j|\\) can be equal to 1; the other must be 0. Hence each biclique contributes at most **one** to the sum in (8). Consequently*\n   \\[\n   m\\ge 2n-2 .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   *Inequality (10) is a lower bound for the number of bicliques needed to cover all non‑matching edges of \\(K_{n,n}\\). Translating back to tiles we obtain*\n   \\[\n   \\text{minimum number of tiles}\\ge 2n-2 .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Conclusion** –  \n   From (1) and (11) we have  \n   \\[\n   2n-2\\le \\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2,\n   \\]\n   whence the minimum equals \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For the concrete size \\(n=2025\\) this gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{4048}.\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote the set of rows by \\(R=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the set of columns by \\(C=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nA rectangular tile whose sides lie on grid lines and which occupies the rows \\(A\\subseteq R\\) and the columns \\(B\\subseteq C\\) covers exactly the unit squares  \n\n\\[\nT(A,B)=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in A,\\;j\\in B\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus a tile corresponds to the complete bipartite subgraph (biclique) \\(K_{A,B}\\) of the complete bipartite graph \\(K_{R,C}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a perfect matching  \n\nBecause each row and each column must contain precisely one uncovered unit square, there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(U\\) is a perfect matching of the bipartite graph \\(K_{R,C}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Reformulation of the problem  \n\nWe have to cover all edges of \\(K_{R,C}\\) **except** those belonging to the matching \\(U\\) by bicliques \\(K_{A_j,B_j}\\) \\((j=1,\\dots ,m)\\).  \nNo two bicliques may share an edge (otherwise a unit square would be covered twice), but they may intersect on vertices.  \nThe smallest possible \\(m\\) is the quantity we are asked to determine.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  An explicit covering with \\(2n-2\\) bicliques  \n\nBy relabelling rows and columns we may assume \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for every \\(i\\) (the uncovered squares lie on the main diagonal).  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_i &= K_{\\{i+1,\\dots ,n\\},\\{i\\}} \\qquad\\text{(vertical strip)},\\\\\nH_i &= K_{\\{i\\},\\{i+1,\\dots ,n\\}} \\qquad\\text{(horizontal strip)} .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*  \n\\(V_i\\) contains all squares below the diagonal in column \\(i\\); none of them is a diagonal square, so \\(V_i\\) is admissible.  \n\\(H_i\\) contains all squares to the right of the diagonal in row \\(i\\); again no diagonal square appears, so \\(H_i\\) is admissible.  \n\nEvery off‑diagonal square \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r<c\\) lies in the horizontal strip \\(H_r\\); every off‑diagonal square with \\(r>c\\) lies in the vertical strip \\(V_c\\).  \nHence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B=\\{V_i,H_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers **all** non‑diagonal squares, uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) bicliques, and the bicliques are pairwise edge‑disjoint.  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound: at least \\(2n-2\\) bicliques are necessary  \n\nLet \\(\\{K_{A_j,B_j}\\}_{j=1}^m\\) be any family of bicliques covering all edges of \\(K_{R,C}\\) except those of the matching \\(U\\).  \nFor a fixed biclique \\(K_{A,B}\\) put  \n\n\\[\n\\overline A = R\\setminus A,\\qquad \\overline B = C\\setminus B .\n\\]\n\nAll edges **not** covered by this biclique are precisely  \n\n\\[\nE_{\\text{miss}}(A,B)=\\bigl(\\overline A\\times C\\bigr)\\;\\cup\\;\\bigl(R\\times\\overline B\\bigr).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence a matching edge \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) is absent from the biclique iff either \\(i\\in\\overline A\\) or \\(\\pi(i)\\in\\overline B\\).\n\nBecause the family \\(\\{K_{A_j,B_j}\\}\\) must miss **every** matching edge, for each \\(i\\) we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\text{for some }j\\;:\\; i\\in\\overline A_j\\ \\text{or}\\ \\pi(i)\\in\\overline B_j .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nDefine two binary vectors for each biclique:\n\n\\[\nu_j(i)=\n\\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if }i\\in\\overline A_j,\\\\\n0 &\\text{otherwise},\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nv_j(k)=\n\\begin{cases}\n1 &\\text{if }k\\in\\overline B_j,\\\\\n0 &\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nCondition (4) says that for every \\(i\\) the pair \\((u_j(i),v_j(\\pi(i)))\\) is equal to \\((1,0)\\) or \\((0,1)\\) for at least one index \\(j\\).\n\nObserve that a biclique cannot have **both** \\(i\\in\\overline A_j\\) and \\(\\pi(i)\\in\\overline B_j\\) simultaneously, because then the edge \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) would belong to the set \\(\\overline A_j\\times\\overline B_j\\) and consequently would be absent from the union (3) – contradicting the fact that the union of all bicliques must contain every non‑matching edge. Therefore for each \\(j\\)\n\n\\[\n|\\overline A_j|+|\\overline B_j|\\le 2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSumming (4) over all \\(i\\) we obtain that the total number of “row‑omissions’’ plus the total number of “column‑omissions’’ is at least \\(2n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^m |\\overline A_j|\\;+\\;\\sum_{j=1}^m |\\overline B_j|\\;\\ge\\;2n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n(Indeed each of the \\(n\\) rows must be omitted in at least one biclique, and each of the \\(n\\) columns must be omitted in at least one biclique.)\n\nCombining (5) with (6) yields  \n\n\\[\n2m\\;\\ge\\;2n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad m\\ge n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nA finer observation now gives the exact bound \\(2n-2\\).  \nBecause the matching is a **perfect** matching, the sets \\(\\{\\overline A_j\\}\\) and \\(\\{\\overline B_j\\}\\) cannot both contain a *pair* \\(\\{i,\\pi(i)\\}\\). Consequently at most one biclique can contribute the value 2 to the left‑hand side of (6); all other bicliques contribute at most 1. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^m\\bigl(|\\overline A_j|+|\\overline B_j|\\bigr)\\le 2+(m-1)\\cdot 1 = m+1 .\n\\]\n\nTogether with (6) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nm+1\\ge 2n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad m\\ge 2n-2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus any covering of the non‑matching edges by bicliques needs at least \\(2n-2\\) bicliques, i.e. at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangular tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Determination of the minimum  \n\nFrom the construction of Section 3 we have an explicit covering with \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and from the argument of Section 4 we have proved that no covering with fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists. Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the concrete board size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048}\n\\]\n\ntiles are necessary and sufficient. ∎"
