) and column \\(c\\).\n\nA **tile** is an axis\u2011parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; equivalently a tile is a set  \n\n\\[\nR=R_{\\text{rows}}\\times R_{\\text{cols}},\n\\qquad\nR_{\\text{rows}}=\\{a,a+1,\\dots ,b\\},\n\\;\nR_{\\text{cols}}=\\{c,c+1,\\dots ,d\\},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).  \nTwo distinct tiles must be disjoint (no unit square may be covered twice).\n\nMatilda must leave exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the smallest possible number of tiles in a partition of \\(G\\setminus U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\cup\\{(i+1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|S|=2n-2\\).  Every element of \\(S\\) lies off the diagonal, so it must belong to some tile.\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Claim.}\\quad\\text{No tile can contain two distinct points of }S.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nLet a tile be the rectangle \\(R=R_{\\text{rows}}\\times R_{\\text{cols}}\\) with\n\\(R_{\\text{rows}}=[a,b]\\) and \\(R_{\\text{cols}}=[c,d]\\) (interval notation for integer sets).  \nAssume that \\(R\\) contains two different points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Case 1.* Both points are of the form \\((i,i+1)\\) (above the diagonal).  \nThen we have \\((i,i+1)\\in R\\) and \\((j,j+1)\\in R\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nHence \\(a\\le i<j\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le i+1<j+1\\le d\\).  \nBecause \\(i+1\\le j\\), the column \\(j\\) belongs to \\([c,d]\\) and the row \\(j\\) belongs to \\([a,b]\\); therefore the diagonal cell \\((j,j)\\) lies in \\(R\\).  This diagonal cell must be uncovered, contradicting the definition 