ch hole, plus left/right of each hole? Actually, each cell is either above its column's hole (i pi(c)) or below (i > pi(c)). So the complement is partitioned into two monotone regions: the region above the permutation diagram (a Young diagram shape) and the region below. The shape is like a Ferrers diagram defined by the permutation.\n\nWe can partition the region above the permutation diagram into rectangles using a \"staircase\" decomposition. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a Young diagram shape (Ferrers shape) is equal to the number of \"inner corners\" or something like that. For a permutation shape, the number of rectangles needed to partition the region above the diagram is equal to the number of rows (or columns) where the shape changes. Actually, we can partition the region above the diagram into at most n rectangles, but maybe less.\n\nLet's consider the identity permutation (holes on main diagonal). The region above the diagonal consists of all cells (i, j) with i j. This region is a triangular shape. It can be partitioned into n-1 rectangles: For each k from 1 to n-1, the rectangle covering rows 1..k and columns k+1..n (i.e., a rectangle of size k x (n-k)). This covers all cells above the diagonal exactly once. That's n-1 rectangles. Similarly, the region below the diagonal can be partitioned into n-1 rectangles: For each k from 2 to n, rectangle covering rows k..n and columns 1..k-1 (size (n-k+1) x (k-1)). So total 2n-2 rectangles. That's exactly the solution's construction (vertical strips per column is another way). But we can also partition the above-diagonal region into n-1 rectangles as described, which is the same count.\n\nBut can we do better than n-1 rectangles for the region above the diagonal? The region above the diagonal is a triangular shape; the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a triangular shape (strictly upper triangular matrix) is indeed n-1. Because each rectangle can only cover a contiguous block of rows and columns, and the shape has a \"staircase\" boundary. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a Young diagram shape is equal to the number of \"steps\" in the shape, which for the identity permutation is n-1. So we cannot partition the region above the diagonal with fewer than n-1 rectangles. Similarly for below. So total at least 2n-2. So the lower bound might be correct for the identity permutation case. However, the solution's lower bound argument was flawed but the conclusion may still hold for the worst-case permutation. The problem asks for the minimum number of tiles needed to guarantee the condition for any arrangement of holes? Actually, the problem: \"Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of different sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\"\n\nInterpretation: Matilda can choose the placement of holes (uncovered squares) as part of the tiling? The problem states \"Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles ... Determine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\" It does not say that the holes are predetermined; she can decide which squares remain uncovered, as long as each row and column has exactly one uncovered square. So she can choose the permutation to minimize the number of tiles needed. So the problem is to find the minimal number of tiles needed for some arrangement of holes (i.e., we can choose the permutation to minimize the number of tiles needed). The solution chooses a permutation (identity) that yields exactly 2n-2 tiles. But perhaps there is a permutation that allows fewer tiles. For instance, if we choose holes all in the same row? No, each row must have exactly one hole, so holes form a permutation. Could we choose a permutation that yields a shape that can be tiled with fewer rectangles? For example, if we choose holes all in the same column? Not allowed because each column must have exactly one hole, so it's a permutation.\n\nWe need to find the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix, where we can choose the permutation to minimize the number of rectangles. The solution chooses identity, which yields 2n-2 rectangles. But perhaps there exists a permutation that yields fewer rectangles. Let's explore.\n\nWe need to partition the complement of a permutation matrix into axis-aligned rectangles (tiles) that are disjoint and cover all squares except the holes. The number of rectangles needed depends on the shape of the permutation diagram. For some permutations, the shape may be \"more balanced\" and allow fewer rectangles. For example, consider the permutation that is a \"reverse\" (holes on anti-diagonal). The region above the anti-diagonal is also a triangular shape, same count. So also 2n-2.\n\nBut perhaps there is a permutation that yields a shape that can be partitioned into fewer rectangles. For instance, consider the permutation that is a \"single cycle\" that is monotone? Actually any permutation yields a shape that is a set of points dividing the grid into two monotone regions: above and below. The region above the permutation diagram is a Young diagram shape defined by the permutation's \"inversion diagram\". The number of rectangles needed to partition a Young diagram shape is equal to the number of \"corners\" (i.e., number of rows where the shape's length changes). The shape is defined by the sequence of row lengths: For each row i, the number of squares above the hole in that row is r_i - 1? Wait, we need to define shape above holes: For each row i, the hole is at column p(i). So squares in that row to the left of the hole are not necessarily above or below; they are left of the hole. Actually, the region above the holes is defined column-wise: For each column c, squares above the hole are rows 1..r_c-1. So the shape above is defined by column heights h_c = r_c - 1. So the shape above is a skyline shape: For each column c, we have a vertical stack of height h_c. So the shape above is a \"histogram\" shape. Similarly, the shape below is a histogram with heights n - r_c.\n\nThus the region above is a union of columns of varying heights. Partitioning this region into rectangles can be done by merging adjacent columns where heights allow a rectangle. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a histogram shape is equal to the number of \"maximal contiguous intervals of columns with non-increasing heights\"? Actually, there is known result: The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a skyline shape (histogram) is equal to the number of \"steps\" in the skyline, i.e., the number of times the height changes when scanning left to right, plus maybe something else. Let's think: Suppose we have heights h_1, h_2, ..., h_n (non-negative integers). The region above is the set of squares (i, c) with i <= h_c. We want to partition this region into axis-aligned rectangles that are disjoint and cover all squares. This is essentially partitioning a skyline shape into rectangles. The minimal number of rectangles needed is equal to the number of \"maximal rectangles\" that can be formed by merging columns of equal height. But we can also combine columns of different heights by using rectangles that stop at the lower height. For example, consider heights [3,2,1]. The region above is shape like a staircase. Minimal rectangles: we can partition into 3 rectangles: one covering rows 1..1 columns 1..3 (height 1 across all columns), one covering rows 2..2 columns 1..2 (height 2 across first two columns), one covering rows 3..3 column 1 (height 3). That's exactly number of distinct heights