\\(\\mathcal L\\) (tiles contained in \\(\\bigcup_i L_i\\)) and a **right\n   family** \\(\\mathcal R\\) (tiles contained in \\(\\bigcup_i R_i\\)).  The two\n   families are disjoint, so  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\text{tiles}|=|\\mathcal L|+|\\mathcal R|.\n   \\]\n\n   *Left side.*  \n   Consider the rows with a non‑empty left block, i.e. those with\n   \\(\\pi(i)>1\\).  List them as \\(i_1,i_2,\\dots ,i_{k}\\) with\n   \\(\\pi(i_1)<\\pi(i_2)<\\dots <\\pi(i_k)\\); here \\(k=n-1\\) because exactly\n   one row (the one with \\(\\pi(i)=1\\)) has an empty left block.\n   For each \\(t\\) the cell  \n\n   \\[\n   c_t=(i_t,\\;\\pi(i_t)-1)\\in L_{i_t}\n   \\]\n\n   is the rightmost cell of the left block of row \\(i_t\\).\n   Any tile of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that appears **before** row \\(i_t\\) and also\n   meets \\(L_{i_t}\\) must have its column interval contained in\n   \\(\\{1,\\dots ,\\pi(i_t)-2\\}\\); otherwise it would contain the hole\n   \\((i_t,\\pi(i_t))\\).  Hence such a tile cannot cover \\(c_t\\).  Therefore\n   a new tile of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is forced for each \\(t\\), and the tiles\n   required for distinct \\(t\\) are distinct (their column intervals end at\n   different values \\(\\pi(i_t)-1\\)).  Thus  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal L|\\ge k=n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   *Right side.*  \n   The argument is symmetric.  List the rows with a non‑empty right block,\n   i.e. those with \\(\\pi(j)<n\\), as \\(j_1,j_2,\\dots ,j_{\\ell}\\) with\n   \\(\\pi(j_1)>\\pi(j_2)>\\dots >\\pi(j_{\\ell})\\); again \\(\\ell=n-1\\).\n   For each \\(t\\) the cell  \n\n   \\[\n   d_t=(j_t,\\;\\pi(j_t)+1)\\in R_{j_t}\n   \\]\n\n   is the leftmost cell of the right block of row \\(j_t\\).\n   Any earlier tile of \\(\\mathcal R\\) meeting \\(R_{j_t}\\) must have its\n   column interval contained in \\(\\{\\pi(j_t)+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so it cannot\n   cover \\(d_t\\).  Consequently a new tile of \\(\\mathcal R\\) is forced for\n   each \\(t\\), giving  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal R|\\ge \\ell=n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Adding the two independent bounds yields  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\text{tiles}|\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The explicit tiling of step 2 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and step 3\n   proves that no tiling can use fewer.  Hence the minimum possible number\n   of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit\nsquare, the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix: there exists\na bijection \\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the unique\nuncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\).  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(r,j):j+1\\le r\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal tile  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,c):i+1\\le c\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\).\n\n*Disjointness.*  \n\\(V_j\\) occupies column \\(j\\) and rows \\(>j\\); \\(H_i\\) occupies row \\(i\\)\nand columns \\(>i\\).  A cell could belong to both a vertical and a\nhorizontal tile only if it satisfied \\(r>c\\) and \\(c>r\\) simultaneously,\nwhich is impossible.  Hence the tiles are pairwise disjoint.\n\n*Avoidance of uncovered squares.*  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  None of the\nvertical tiles contains a cell with column equal to its row index, because\n\\(V_j\\) uses rows \\(>j\\).  None of the horizontal tiles contains a cell\nwith row equal to its column index, because \\(H_i\\) uses columns \\(>i\\).\nThus every diagonal cell remains uncovered.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery non‑diagonal cell either lies strictly below the di