d for each\n\\(t\\).  The tiles forced for different values of \\(t\\) are distinct,\nbecause their rightmost columns \\(\\pi(i_t)-1\\) are distinct.  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L|\\ge k=n-1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  At least \\(n-1\\) right‑side tiles\n\nThe symmetric argument deals with rows having a non‑empty right block,\ni.e. those with \\(\\pi(j)<n\\); there are again \\(n-1\\) of them.  List them\nas  \n\n\\[\nj_1,j_2,\\dots ,j_{\\ell}\\qquad(\\ell=n-1)\n\\]\n\nwith  \n\n\\[\n\\pi(j_1)>\\pi(j_2)>\\dots >\\pi(j_{\\ell}).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(t\\) the cell  \n\n\\[\nd_t=(j_t,\\;\\pi(j_t)+1)\\in R_{j_t}\n\\]\n\nis the leftmost cell of the right block of row \\(j_t\\).\nIf a tile \\(V\\in\\mathcal R\\) that appears before row \\(j_t\\) meets a cell\nof \\(R_{j_t}\\), its column interval must be contained in\n\\([\\pi(j_t)+2,\\,n]\\); otherwise it would also contain the hole\n\\((j_t,\\pi(j_t))\\).  Hence such a tile cannot cover \\(d_t\\), and a new\ntile of \\(\\mathcal R\\) is forced for each \\(t\\).  Distinct values of \\(t\\)\nyield distinct tiles, giving  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal R|\\ge \\ell=n-1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Total lower bound\n\nCombining the two independent estimates,\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}=|\\mathcal L|+|\\mathcal R|\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Minimality\n\nThe construction of Section 1 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the\ninequality of Section 2 shows that no tiling can use fewer.  Therefore the\nminimum possible number of tiles for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid is  \n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
