the two independent bounds yields  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\text{tiles}|\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The explicit tiling of step\u202f2 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and step\u202f3\n   proves that no tiling can use fewer.  Hence the minimum possible number\n   of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit\nsquare, the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix: there exists\na bijection \\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the unique\nuncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\).  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(r,j):j+1\\le r\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal tile  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,c):i+1\\le c\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\).\n\n*Disjointness.*  \n\\(V_j\\) occupies column \\(j\\) and rows \\(>j\\); \\(H_i\\) occupies row \\(i\\)\nand columns \\(>i\\).  A cell could belong to both a vertical and a\nhorizontal tile only if it satisfied \\(r>c\\) and \\(c>r\\) simultaneously,\nwhich is impossible.  Hence the tiles are pairwise disjoint.\n\n*Avoidance of uncovered squares.*  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  None of the\nvertical tiles contains a cell with column equal to its row index, because\n\\(V_j\\) uses rows \\(>j\\).  None of the horizontal tiles contains a cell\nwith row equal to its column index, because \\(H_i\\) uses columns \\(>i\\).\nThus every diagonal cell remains uncovered.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery non\u2011diagonal cell either lies strictly below the diagonal (so it is\nin some \\(V_j\\)) or strictly above the diagonal (so it is in some\n\\(H_i\\)).  Consequently all unit squares except the diagonal are covered.\n\nThe number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n| \\{V_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}|+| \\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A universal lower bound\n\nFor an arbitrary permutation \\(\\pi\\) define, for each row \\(i\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad\nR_i=\\{(i,j):\\pi(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA tile that meets a cell of \\(L_i\\) cannot meet a cell of \\(R_i\\), because\notherwise it would also contain the hole \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  Hence the set of\nall tiles splits into two disjoint families:\n\n* \\(\\mathcal L\\): tiles contained entirely in \\(\\bigcup_i L_i\\);\n* \\(\\mathcal R\\): tiles contained entirely in \\(\\bigcup_i R_i\\).\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}=|\\mathcal L|+|\\mathcal R|.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  At least \\(n-1\\) left\u2011side tiles\n\nRows with a non\u2011empty left block are precisely those with \\(\\pi(i)>1\\);\nthere are \\(n-1\\) of them.  List these rows as  \n\n\\[\ni_1,i_2,\\dots ,i_{k}\\qquad(k=n-1)\n\\]\n\nso that  \n\n\\[\n\\pi(i_1)<\\pi(i_2)<\\dots <\\pi(i_k).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(t\\) consider the cell  \n\n\\[\nc_t=(i_t,\\;\\pi(i_t)-1)\\in L_{i_t},\n\\]\n\nthe rightmost cell of the left block of row \\(i_t\\).\n\nSuppose a tile \\(U\\in\\mathcal L\\) that appears **before** row \\(i_t\\)\ncovers a cell of \\(L_{i_t}\\).  Because \\(U\\) is a rectangle, its column\ninterval is an interval \\([a,b]\\).  If \\(b\\ge\\pi(i_t)-1\\), then column\n\\(\\pi(i_t)\\) would also belong to the interval, forcing \\(U\\) to contain\nthe hole \\((i_t,\\pi(i_t))\\), which is impossible.  Hence any such tile\nmust satisfy \\(b\\le\\pi(i_t)-2\\) and therefore cannot cover the cell\n\\(c_t\\).  Consequently a new tile of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is required for each\n\\(t\\).  The tiles forced for different values of \\(t\\) are distinct,\nbecause their rightmost columns \\(\\pi(i_t)-1\\) are distinct.  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L|\\ge k=n-1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  At least \\(n-1\\) right\u2011side tiles\n\nThe symmetric argument deals with rows having a non\u2011empty right block,\ni.e. those with \\(\\pi(j)<n\\); there are again \\(n-1\\) of them.  List them\nas  \n\n\\[\nj_1,j_2,\\dots ,j_{\\ell}\\qquad(\\ell=n-1)\n\\]\n\nwith  \n\n\\[\n\\pi(j_1)>\\pi(j_2)>\\dots >\\pi(j_{\\ell}).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(t\\) the cell  \n\n\\[\nd_t=(j_t,\\;\\pi(j_t)+1)\\in R_{j_t}\n\\]\n\nis the leftmost cell of the right block of row \\(j_t\\).\nIf a tile \\(V\\in\\mathcal R\\) that appears before row \\(j_t\\) meets a cell\nof \\(R_{j_t}\\), its column interval must be contained in\n\\([\\pi(j_t)+2,\\,n]\\); otherwise it would also contain the hole\n\\((j_t,\\pi(j_t))\\).  Hence such a tile cannot cover \\(d_t\\), and a new\ntile of \\(\\mathcal R\\) is forced for each \\(t\\).  Distinct values of \\(t\\)\nyield distinct tiles, giving  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal R|\\ge \\ell=n-1 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Total lower bound\n\nCombining the two independent estimates,\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}=|\\mathcal L|+|\\mathcal R|\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Minimality\n\nThe construction of Section\u202f1 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the\ninequality of Section\u202f2 shows that no tiling can use fewer.  Therefore the\nminimum possible number of tiles for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid is  \n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
